    "id": "lb7-longrange",
    "name": "7\" lifting body",
    "blurb": "7-inch long-range quad with a 345 mm wing across the frame, rigged 32° up from the props. 1.2 kg on 6S2P Li-ion: the wing carries about a quarter of the weight at cruise and the range goes up by a quarter with it.",
    "notes": "Assumed: 1,200 g all-up with the wing and the pack; 1,500 gf per motor on 6S from a 2806.5-class motor on a 7x3.5; 6S2P of P30B-class 18650s at 23 mOhm a cell. The lifting body is a 345 mm symmetrical plate between the arms, 150 mm at the root tapering to 110, swept 14 degrees, rigged 32 degrees leading-edge-up from the plane of the props. That last number is the one that matters: a quad leans nose-down to fly forward, so a wing flat in the prop plane would push down. At the 19-degree cruise the sim benches, this wing flies at 13 degrees and carries about a quarter of the weight. Weigh yours and put a real thrust figure in.",
